  "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u},\\,u=4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/4} & ={(20{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =20e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={(5{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =5{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5e & =20e(x-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ y-5e & =20ex-5e \\\\ y & =20ex\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
